data.content:
Now I have the full picture. Here's the explanation:

## What happened

C# 14 introduces new **span overload resolution rules**. When you write `array.Contains(num)` on an `int[]`, the compiler now binds to `MemoryExtensions.Contains(Span<T>, T)` instead of `Enumerable.Contains<T>(IEnumerable<T>, T)`.

The `Span`-based overload is **not representable in expression trees** (expression trees can't contain span types), so it throws at runtime when the expression is compiled/interpreted.

## How to fix

Force the compiler to pick the `Enumerable` overload by casting the array to `IEnumerable<int>`:

```csharp
// Option A: .AsEnumerable()
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.AsEnumerable().Contains(num);

// Option B: explicit cast
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => ((IEnumerable<int>)array).Contains(num);

// Option C: call Enumerable.Contains directly
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);
```

Any of these prevent the compiler from choosing the `MemoryExtensions.Contains` span overload, keeping the expression tree valid.
